    <h1>What the R-22 Phase-Out Means for Your Old HVAC System</h1>
    <p class="intro-lead">
      If a technician looks at your outdoor air conditioner or heat pump and points out that it runs on <strong>R-22</strong> (often sold under the legacy brand name Freon), your repair-vs-replace math changes immediately. That is why our calculator includes a dedicated hard flag whenever a system is over 12 years old and uses R-22.
    </p>

    <h2>Verified Federal Refrigerant Timeline (U.S. EPA)</h2>
    <div class="source-callout">
      <ul style="margin-bottom: 0;">
        <li><strong>2010:</strong> Under U.S. EPA Clean Air Act regulations, manufacturing and importing <strong>new</strong> air conditioning and heat pump equipment charged with R-22 was banned effective January 1, 2010.</li>
        <li><strong>January 1, 2020:</strong> The EPA completely banned the <strong>production and import</strong> of virgin R-22 in the United States.</li>
        <li><strong>2020–Present:</strong> Existing R-22 units can still be serviced legally, but <strong>only recovered, recycled, or reclaimed R-22</strong> from existing equipment stock remains available for servicing.</li>
        <li><strong>AIM Act Transition (R-410A → R-454B / R-32):</strong> Under the EPA’s American Innovation and Manufacturing (AIM) Act, HFC refrigerants including R-410A are now being phased down as well, and new residential split systems transition to lower-GWP refrigerants such as <strong>R-454B</strong> and <strong>R-32</strong>.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h2>Why R-22 Leaks Destroy Repair Economics</h2>
    <p>
      A sealed air conditioner should never consume refrigerant. When an R-22 unit is low on charge, there is a physical pinhole leak in the copper evaporator coil, condenser coil, flare fitting, or line set. Repairing that problem creates three compounding risks on any system built in or before 2009:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Shrinking reclaimed supply:</strong> Because no new R-22 has been manufactured or imported into the US since January 1, 2020, technicians rely exclusively on reclaimed cylinders recovered from decommissioned equipment. Every pound added to a leaking coil carries scarcity pricing.</li>
      <li><strong>A “recharge only” buys months, not years:</strong> Spending $600 to $1,100 to top off a leaking coil without brazing or replacing the coil simply leaks that expensive reclaimed gas back outside—often before the end of the cooling season.</li>
      <li><strong>Dry-shipped R-22 replacement parts are a dead end:</strong> Even if a tech replaces a major R-22 component using a dry-nitrogen “dry unit,” the older indoor coil or compressor sitting alongside it is still well past 15 years old and carries no long-term system warranty.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>How to Verify What Refrigerant Your Unit Uses</h2>
    <p>
      Do not guess from memory. Walk outside to the metal nameplate (data plate) bolted to the side of your condenser or heat pump cabinet and look for two lines:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Refrigerant stamp:</strong> It will explicitly print <code>R-22</code> (HCFC-22), <code>R-410A</code>, <code>R-32</code>, or <code>R-454B</code> along with factory charge in ounces or pounds.</li>
      <li><strong>Manufacture date:</strong> Look for <code>MFR DATE</code> or read the first four digits of the serial number. Any residential split AC manufactured in 2010 or later cannot legally be factory-charged with R-22 (except limited legacy dry-shipped service condensers).</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What About R-410A Under the AIM Act?</h2>
    <p>
      Homeowners often confuse R-22 with R-410A after hearing headlines about refrigerant changes. Here is the key distinction:
    </p>
    <p>
      R-22 is an ozone-depleting HCFC whose production and import ended completely on <strong>January 1, 2020</strong>. By contrast, <strong>R-410A</strong> is an HFC being phased down gradually on a multi-year schedule under the EPA’s AIM Act, with new residential systems shifting to A2L refrigerants like <strong>R-454B</strong> and <strong>R-32</strong>. If you currently own an 8-year-old R-410A system that needs a $450 capacitor or blower motor, you do <em>not</em> need to panic-replace it—R-410A service supply remains widely available for servicing existing systems.
    </p>

    <h2>When Replacing an R-22 Unit, Watch the Line Set</h2>
    <p>
      One of the easiest ways an R-22 replacement quote goes wrong is reusing old copper refrigerant tubing without checking oil compatibility and pipe diameter:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>R-22 systems circulated <strong>mineral oil</strong>; modern R-410A, R-454B, and R-32 systems circulate <strong>POE (polyolester) synthetic oil</strong>. Leftover mineral oil and acidic sludge inside an old line set can damage a brand-new compressor or clog a metering device.</li>
      <li>Modern high-efficiency condensers often require a larger suction line diameter than 1990s/2000s R-22 units.</li>
      <li>Always confirm in writing whether your replacement bid includes installing a <strong>new copper line set</strong> (or, where lines are buried behind finished drywall, a documented manufacturer-approved chemical flush and pressure test).</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Worked Example: R-22 Coil Leak vs. New System Math</h2>
    <div class="worked-example">
      <p>
        <strong>Scenario:</strong> A homeowner in Georgia has a <strong>16-year-old R-22 split AC</strong> that stopped cooling on a 92°F afternoon. The technician finds a leaking indoor evaporator coil and gives two quotes:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Repair option:</strong> Replace evaporator coil + recover/recharge reclaimed R-22 = <strong>$2,450</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>Replace option:</strong> Install new matched 14.3 SEER2 R-454B/R-32 condenser, coil, and new copper line set = <strong>$9,600</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>Homeowner stay horizon:</strong> 6 years.</li>
      </ul>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">
        <strong>Why the 4-check audit leans replace:</strong> Check A (<code>16 × $2,450 = $39,200</code>) fires heavily over $5,000. Check C (age &gt; 12 AND R-22) fires the hard flag. Even though $2,450 is 25.5% of $9,600 (under the 50% rule), sinking $2,450 into a 16-year-old outdoor compressor running obsolete R-22 leaves zero protection if the outdoor compressor fails next summer—turning total 6-year spend into $2,450 + $9,600 = <strong>$12,050</strong> instead of $9,600 once.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>Check Your Numbers Before Deciding</h2>
    <p>
      If your system is older than 12 years and stamped R-22, run your exact quotes through the <a href="/">HVAC Repair or Replace Calculator</a> and review <a href="/guides/how-to-read-hvac-quote/">our quote checklist</a> to make sure any replacement bid explicitly covers the line set and EPA Section 608 recovery.
    </p>
